# `arvo-format`: the declared signature

**Canon this design declares.** `ruling::the_format_spine_is_canon` and the four propositions it
ratifies, chiefly `proposal::membership_of_the_representable_set_is_one_affine_predicate` and
`proposal::arithmetic_on_a_format_factors_as_an_adaptation_of_an_exact_operation`.
`ruling::behaviour_is_stated_per_declared_signature_and_the_premise_dissolves`.
`ruling::the_derivation_is_a_placement_and_the_operation_set_is_an_admission_rule`, for the admission
rule half. `ruling::the_ambiguous_rounding_word_is_retired_for_six_explicit_names`.
`ruling::the_overflow_panic_is_permitted_and_bounded`.
`ruling::never_a_runtime_check_and_one_lowered_path`.
`ruling::the_standard_is_parity_in_output_not_in_the_internals`.

## What this crate is

The declared signature, and nothing else. A declared signature is a format paired with an adaptation,
and it is what every operation arvo declares is a function of. The carrier is not here and cannot be
reached from here, because this crate does not depend on the crate that has one.

That absence is the design rather than an omission. The floor says every operation the design declares
is a function of the declared width and never of the machine carrier, and a dependency edge is the
only way to state that so it cannot quietly stop being true.

## The representable set is one predicate, not a family of them

A format is identified by its ambient domain and its representable set, and the representable set is a
constant of the type. Membership is one affine predicate over one parameterisation: a slot function, a
quantum per magnitude, and a phase.

For a value `v` in the ambient domain, `v` is representable exactly when there is a magnitude `m` and
a slot `i` within the slot range at `m` such that

```
v = phase + i * quantum(m)
```

Integers, fixed point, scaled integers and floats are points of that one predicate rather than four
concepts:

| Point | Quantum | Phase | Magnitudes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Integers | `1`, constant | zero | one |
| Fixed point at fraction width `F` | `radix^-F`, constant | zero | one |
| Scaled integers at scale `s` | `s`, constant | zero, or nonzero when biased | one |
| Floats | `radix^(m - precision + 1)`, indexed by magnitude | zero | many |

Subnormals are not a case. They fall out of taking the maximum at the smallest magnitude, which is
what the ratified sentence says and is the test that the unification is real rather than a
presentation.

**The phase is carried explicitly and is never assumed zero.** The canon's reason travels with it: a
nonzero phase decides whether the identity adaptation ever occurs and whether the set carries an
additive identity at all. A half-step-biased grid is not closed under exact addition, contains neither
zero nor one, and is not even a monoid carrier. A design that hardcodes the phase to zero has closed
that case silently, which is why the coordinate is present even though every point in the table above
except one biased case sets it to zero.

## The quantum law is where the two families differ, and it is the only place

`Quantum` is the axis that separates the constant-quantum family from the magnitude-indexed one. Every
other coordinate is shared. So a claim about one family is a claim predicated on the quantum law, and
the crate carries that as a named axis rather than as two parallel hierarchies.

This is what makes the cross-kind arithmetic question a question about one space rather than about an
interface between two. It is also where that space is known to be awkward: within one kind the points
form a lattice, and putting both kinds in one space breaks the join on pairs that are both
fixed-point, because a floating point can sit between two fixed points and be incomparable with the
one that used to be the join. That is a measured result and it is open as
`question::is_the_cross_kind_join_closed_or_priced`. **This crate does not close it and does not
depend on it being closed**: nothing here needs a join.

## The adaptation is a first-class object

Arithmetic on a format is an exact operation in the ambient domain composed with a named, total
adaptation onto the representable set. The adaptation is a thing with its own laws, not a flag on an
operation, and an operation that fuses one invisibly is an operation and an adaptation point wearing
one name.

So the crate names two independent coordinates of an adaptation.

**The rounding mode**, which decides what happens between grid points. Six names, and this is the one
closed enumeration in the design, closed because a ratification closed it:

`toward_zero`, `floor`, `ceil`, `half_up`, `half_even`, `stochastic`.

Both spellings of the retired word are gone and do not appear anywhere in this crate. The reason
travels with the vocabulary: on a signed domain that word named two operations that genuinely differ.
Bit truncation of a two's complement value is `floor`, and it is not `toward_zero`; the note exists so
the hardware operation is not read back into the name.

**The overflow policy**, which decides what happens outside the representable range. This is an open
inventory, not a closed set. `wrap`, `saturate` and `clamp` are the members the corpus carries;
another joins by supplying the obligations.

The two are independent because they answer different questions, and the realisation map is one map
with two regions rather than two mechanisms: rounding between grid points, completion outside the
range. A magnitude bound switches off the completion and not the rounding, and a grid bound does the
reverse, which is what two regions of one map predict and what two independent mechanisms do not.

### The panic is not an overflow policy, and this is a derived call

**Derived, marked as derived, from two ratified rows that agree.** The ratified factoring requires the
adaptation to be total. A panic diverges, so it is not a total map onto the representable set and
cannot be a member of the slot. From the other direction,
`ruling::the_overflow_panic_is_permitted_and_bounded` puts the panic on the build axis: it is the
go-to for what cannot be caught at compile time through the typestate, on debug builds, in every
strategy, and on release what stands in its place is the guarantee plus an explicit declaration of the
mode, which lowers and behaves accordingly.

So the panic is a property of how an adaptation is applied under a build profile, and it is not one of
the things an adaptation may be. `dimension::build_profile` is the axis that carries it.

`dimension::overflow_policy` still lists `panic` among its values. That disagreement is recorded
rather than repaired here: the dimension set is append-only, so the repair is a note on that row and
not an edit to this design.

The looseness of that ruling is ratified rather than tolerated, in op's own request, so this crate
states the shape and does not sharpen it into a rule about where a panic fires.

## The admission rule, which is how an operation earns its place

The design ships an admission rule rather than an operation list: an operation is admitted exactly
when it is a function of the declared signature, and where two realisations of one name disagree, the
signature is missing a coordinate.

Both halves are load-bearing. The first is the test. The second is what to do when the test fails, and
it says the failure is diagnostic rather than fatal: a name whose two realisations disagree has found
a coordinate nobody declared, and the repair is to declare it.

This is the format spine's closed-concept-open-inventory shape one tier down, which is what makes it a
derivation rather than a new decision.

**What it means for the operation set is that there is not one.** Asking which operations ship is
asking for a list where the rule gives a criterion. What ships is whatever satisfies the rule, and
`ruling::the_standard_is_parity_in_output_not_in_the_internals` is why that is enough: the standards
bound is parity in output for the cases a standard covers, it does not oblige carrying the standard's
operation list, and reaching the result by a different or unconventional route is fine so long as the
logic of the spec is matched.

The worked case is the fused multiply-add. Its result is reachable by composing multiply and add
everywhere except at signed saturating, so it is not a required member of any inventory; it is a
required arm at signed saturating, gated on that region. This crate neither ships that arm nor
forecloses it.

## Where the computation lives, and why it is not in a bound

`generic_const_exprs` is forbidden. `min_generic_const_args` is what there is. So a derived quantity
may not be computed in a bound, and every derived quantity is an associated item resolved by an impl.

Read that as the staging boundary rather than as a restriction worked around. The trait is the
stage-zero object: what is known at monomorphisation. A free `const fn` over a format's associated
items is the stage-zero computation. What the backend sees is stage one, and by then every branch the
computation took is gone. `ruling::never_a_runtime_check_and_one_lowered_path` asks for exactly that,
so the contract form is what the intent wants and the const expression in a bound is the thing that
would have needed erasing.

The consequence for anyone implementing this: membership, the quantum at a magnitude, and the slot
range are `const fn`s taking a format as a type parameter and reading its associated items. They are
not methods on a value and they are not bounds.

## What this crate does not decide

Named rather than filled, because the registry holds each open and a design does not close a canon
question as a side effect of choosing a signature.

`does_warm_wrap_or_clamp`, which is a defaults question about a binding and belongs to
`arvo-strategy`'s inventory rather than here. `is_the_derived_numeral_required_to_be_tightest`, where
soundness is stated and bestness is an arm. `does_precision_count_the_sign_digit`.
`does_narrowing_compose` and `what_the_double_rounding_mechanism_is`, both of which bear on composing
adaptations and neither of which the single-adaptation contract here needs.
`is_the_cross_kind_join_closed_or_priced`, above.

## The laws this crate owes tests for

Stated here because a design that names its laws is what lets the source be checked against something
other than itself, and asserted over the whole matrix of shapes rather than a sample.

**Membership is decidable at const time for every point of the parameterisation**, including a nonzero
phase and a magnitude-indexed quantum.

**The four named points of the table produce the representable sets their conventions document.** This
is the standards bound in its checkable form and it is parity in output.

**Subnormal values fall out of the maximum at the smallest magnitude** with no branch naming them.

**An adaptation is total**: every value of the ambient domain maps to a member of the representable
set, for every rounding mode and every overflow policy. The test that matters is the one that would
fail if a partial member were admitted, which is why the panic is not one.

**Rounding and completion are separable**: a magnitude bound switches off the completion and not the
rounding, and a grid bound does the reverse.

**A nonzero phase removes the additive identity.** The grid contains neither zero nor one, and the
test asserts that rather than treating it as a corner case, because the canon's reason for carrying
the coordinate is that it changes the algebra.
